{"builder":"Goal: when a config file has malformed JSON, the app should still stop with exit 1 and a `bad config:` line on stderr. That line must now also name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. Do NOT make `loadConfig` swallow the error or return `{}`/defaults. Do not change how ENOENT is reported (beyond it staying an exit-1 `bad config:` failure). Do not add schema validation or JSON5/comment support.\n\nRelevant files: `src/config.js` (`loadConfig(path)` is currently `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`), `src/app.js` (catches the error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr, exits 1; otherwise prints `listening on <port>`), `test/config.test.js` (node:test; `npm test` runs `node --test`), `README.md`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file text first, then wrap only the `JSON.parse` call in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error (a SyntaxError is a good choice, so the existing test `bad JSON throws` that asserts `SyntaxError` still passes) whose `message` includes the path exactly as passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path), plus the line and column of the mistake, plus the original parse message. Set `cause` to the original error.\n2. Work out line and column yourself so the result does not depend on the Node version's message format. Take the character position from the original message (e.g. `at position 20`) or, if Node already gives `(line N column M)`, use that. Then compute the 1-based line and column from the file text up to that position (count `\\n`s before the position for the line; column = position minus index of the last `\\n` before it). If no position can be found, still include the path. A clear format is e.g. `<path>:3:3: Expected double-quoted property name in JSON at position 20 (line 3 column 3)`, or `<path>: line 3 column 3: ...`. Both the line number 3 and the column number 3 must appear for the sample below.\n3. Leave the valid-config path unchanged: `loadConfig` on `{\"port\": 9000}` must still return `{ port: 9000 }`, and `node src/app.js ok.json` must still print `listening on 9000` and exit 0. Leave read errors (ENOENT etc.) as they are; they are already reported by `src/app.js`. `src/app.js` probably needs no change, since it prints `error.message`. Make sure nothing is written to stdout on failure.\n4. Add tests to `test/config.test.js` that fail at the current code: (a) `loadConfig` on a temp file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and the thrown error's `message` includes that file's path; (b) for a file with the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, the message includes the path and gives line 3, column 3. Optionally add a test that spawns `node src/app.js <file>` (via `node:child_process` `spawnSync` with `process.execPath`) and asserts exit code 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:` and the path. Keep the existing tests passing.\n5. Optionally update `README.md` to say the message names the file and the line/column.\n6. Check by hand before opening the PR:\n   d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json; echo \"exit $?\"   # expect a stderr line with bad config:, $d/c.json, line 3 and column 3; exit 1\n   printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"   # expect listening on 9000, exit 0\n   npm test   # must pass\n7. Open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head on the repo's Node version (v22). Set up a temp directory first: `d=$(mktemp -d)`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: `printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; cat $d/out; wc -c < $d/out`\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and stdout ($d/out) is empty.\nFAIL: any other exit code, or stdout is not empty (for example it has a `listening on` line).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: `cat $d/err; grep -F 'bad config:' $d/err; grep -F \"$d/c.json\" $d/err`\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string \"$d/c.json\" as passed, and gives line 3 and column 3 of the mistake in some readable form (e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, col 3`).\nFAIL: any of these is missing: `bad config:`, the exact path, line 3, or column 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: `printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: prints `OK`.\nFAIL: prints `RETURNED ...` (the call returned a value) or `NO PATH: ...` (the message lacks the path).\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: `printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, {port: 9000}); console.log('EQUAL', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: the app prints `listening on 9000` on stdout and exits 0, and the second command prints `EQUAL {\"port\":9000}`.\nFAIL: a different stdout or exit code, or the deepEqual assertion throws.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun: `npm test` at the PR head. Then find the merge base of the PR (`git merge-base HEAD <the PR's target ref>`), call it B, and run `git checkout B -- src && npm test; git checkout HEAD -- src` so that the PR's tests run against the base commit's source.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the PR head; with base `src/` at least one test fails, and that failing test checks that the error thrown for malformed config JSON includes the file path (read the test to confirm).\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at the PR head; or every test passes against base `src/`; or none of the failing tests checks for the file path in the malformed-JSON error.\nRestore the working tree afterwards (`git checkout HEAD -- src`, `git status` clean).\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Read `src/config.js` to see whether the line/column are computed from the file text or only taken from Node's own message. Relying only on Node v22's `(line N column M)` text is fragile on other Node versions. Note it if so.\n- Note whether `loadConfig` still throws a SyntaxError (the original `bad JSON throws` test asserts that), whether `cause` keeps the original error, and whether a missing file still gives exit 1 with `bad config:` (`node src/app.js $d/missing.json; echo $?`).\n- Note whether the path is printed as given and not resolved to an absolute path, and whether the README was updated."}